---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Math from '../components/Math.astro';
import { site, personNode } from '../data/site';

const softwareSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'SMVac',
  description:
    'A modular C++/Python framework for precision studies of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model: NNLO matching, 3-loop RG evolution, the one-loop Coleman–Weinberg effective potential, and semiclassical vacuum decay via an RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov calculation.',
  codeRepository: site.smvac,
  author: personNode,
  programmingLanguage: ['C++', 'Python'],
  dateModified: '2026-07-18',
};
---
<Base
  title="SMVac · electroweak vacuum stability"
  description="SMVac: a C++/Python framework for precision studies of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model. 3-loop RG evolution, NNLO matching, the one-loop Coleman–Weinberg potential, and semiclassical vacuum decay via an RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov calculation, with phase diagrams in the (Mh, Mt) plane."
  jsonLd={[softwareSchema]}

  ogImage="/og/smvac.png">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research software · C++ / Python</p>
      <h1>SMVac</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 42rem;">
        A numerical study of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">
        <strong style="color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600;">Naman Goyal</strong> · May 2024 – May 2026 ·
        Mentored by Prof. Rick Sandepan Gupta (TIFR) ·
        <a href={site.smvac} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section prose" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview">
      <h2 id="overview" style="margin-top: 1rem;"><span class="secno">01</span> Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The Standard Model of particle physics appears, from its measured parameters, to live on a
        knife's edge. At field values far beyond any collider, the effective Higgs potential turns
        over: the quartic coupling <Math tex={'\\lambda(\\mu)'} />, run up in energy, becomes negative
        at scales of order <Math tex={'10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. The electroweak vacuum in which
        we live is then not the absolute minimum of the theory but a <em>metastable</em> one,
        destined in principle to decay by quantum tunneling. Whether that decay can ever matter is
        controlled by the semiclassical bounce action <Math tex={'S'} />: the vacuum survives
        cosmological timescales provided <Math tex={'S'} /> is large enough. The measured Higgs and
        top masses place our universe uncomfortably close to the boundary where this guarantee fails.
      </p>
      <p>
        SMVac is the computational framework I developed to study this problem quantitatively. It
        combines precision renormalization group evolution with the semiclassical
        Fubini–Lipatov (conformal) ansatz: rather than assuming a constant quartic coupling, the
        code evaluates the bounce action on the analytical conformal field profile with the full
        running, one-loop effective potential, yielding an <strong>RG-improved decay
        action</strong>. The bounce equation itself is never integrated: the profile family is
        fixed and only its scale is optimized, so the reported action is a variational estimate,
        and the most robust output is the stability boundary, which does not depend on the ansatz.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading"><span class="secno">02</span> The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Why the <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane? The top quark drives <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> down
        through the RG flow, the Higgs mass pushes it up; the stability verdict is therefore a curve
        in the Higgs–top mass plane, and the experimental point
        <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t) \\approx (125.1, 173.1)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> sits close to it.
        Classic analyses (Buttazzo et al. 2013) mapped this boundary with multi-loop precision.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard analytical shortcut evaluates the decay rate in the strict Fubini–Lipatov
        limit, where the potential is assumed purely quartic,
        <Math tex={'V \\approx \\tfrac{1}{4}\\lambda\\,\\phi^4'} /> with <em>constant</em>
        <Math tex={'\\lambda < 0'} /> at high scales. But near the metastability boundary the running
        of the couplings is precisely what matters. The regime where the shortcut is most tempting
        is exactly the regime where it is least reliable, and SMVac quantifies this.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
      <h2 id="method-heading"><span class="secno">03</span> Method</h2>
      <p>The computational pipeline, as implemented in the repository:</p>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li>Input <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> and <Math tex={'\\alpha_s(M_Z)'} /></li>
        <li>NNLO matching at the top scale <Math tex={'M_t'} /> (Buttazzo et al. 2013, linearized)</li>
        <li>3-loop RG evolution to the Planck scale (RK4)</li>
        <li>Locate <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\min}'} /> and the instability scale <Math tex={'\\mu_{\\rm inst}'} /></li>
        <li>RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov action over the conformal profile</li>
        <li>Optimize the profile scale <Math tex={'R'} /> (Golden Section Search)</li>
        <li>Classify via the Coleman criterion <Math tex={'\\Gamma/V \\sim t_U^{-4}'} /></li>
        <li>Repeat over an <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> grid → phase diagram</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Effective potential</h3>
      <p>
        The tree-level potential is improved by the running coupling, with one-loop
        Coleman–Weinberg corrections from the top, <Math tex={'W'} />, and <Math tex={'Z'} /> fields:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'V_{\\rm eff}(\\phi) \\;\\approx\\; \\tfrac{1}{4}\\,\\lambda_{\\rm eff}(\\mu)\\,\\phi^{4} \\;+\\; \\Delta V_{\\rm 1\\text{-}loop}(\\phi), \\qquad \\mu \\sim \\phi.'}
      />

      <h3>Renormalization group evolution</h3>
      <p>
        The couplings <Math tex={'(g_1, g_2, g_3, y_t, \\lambda)'} />, with
        <Math tex={'y_b, y_\\tau'} /> subleading, are evolved with their three-loop beta functions
        by fixed-step RK4 integration in <Math tex={'t = 2\\ln\\mu'} />, from the matching scale
        <Math tex={'M_t'} /> to the Planck scale:
      </p>
      <Math block tex={'\\frac{d g_i}{d \\ln \\mu} \\;=\\; \\beta_{g_i}^{\\rm (3\\text{-}loop)}.'} />

      <h3>Semiclassical decay action</h3>
      <p>
        SMVac retains the analytical Fubini–Lipatov field profile,
        <Math tex={"\\phi_{\\rm FL}(r) = \\sqrt{2/|\\lambda_R|}\\; 2R^{2}/(r^{2}+R^{2})"} />, but
        evaluates the Euclidean action honestly along it: the kinetic term is closed-form in the
        conformal limit, while the potential term is integrated numerically over the full running
        <Math tex={'V_{\\rm eff}'} /> (composite Simpson's rule, <Math tex={'N = 2048'} /> points),
        and the profile radius <Math tex={'R'} /> is optimized by Golden Section Search to tolerance
        <Math tex={'10^{-13}'} />:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'S \\;=\\; \\underbrace{\\frac{16\\pi^{2}}{3\\,|\\lambda_R|}}_{\\rm closed\\ form} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{2\\pi^{2}\\!\\int_0^{\\infty}\\! dr\\; r^{3}\\,\\bigl[V_{\\rm eff}(\\phi_{\\rm FL}) - V_{\\rm eff}(0)\\bigr]}_{\\rm numerical}, \\qquad \\frac{\\Gamma}{V} \\sim A\\,e^{-S}.'}
      />
      <p>
        A point of the plane is classified <em>stable</em> if <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> never turns
        negative, <em>unstable</em> if <Math tex={'S'} /> falls below the universe-age threshold
        <Math tex={'S_{\\rm th} \\approx 4\\ln(T_U\\,\\mu_{\\rm inst})'} />, and
        <em>metastable</em> otherwise. A perturbativity guard flags regions where couplings exceed
        <Math tex={'4\\pi'} />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="results-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">04</span> Results</h2>
        <p>
          Production datasets are evaluated point by point with no interpolation: the full
          <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane at <Math tex={'0.5\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> resolution
          (501 × 501 points), the phenomenologically relevant window at
          <Math tex={'0.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, and a boundary strip at
          <Math tex={'0.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. At the benchmark point
          <Math tex={'(125.1, 173.1)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> the vacuum is <em>metastable</em>:
          <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\rm eff}'} /> crosses zero at
          <Math tex={'\\mu_1 \\simeq 6.2 \\times 10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> and the optimized
          ansatz action is <Math tex={'S_* = 2103.10'} /> against a threshold of
          <Math tex={'483'} />, in agreement with the consensus that the SM lifetime exceeds the
          age of the universe. The absolute-stability boundary sits at
          <Math tex={'M_h^{\\rm crit} = 129.2\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> for
          <Math tex={'M_t = 173.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, within
          <Math tex={'0.6\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> of the published NNLO value, enforced as a band
          check in the test suite.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/fig-full-phase-diagram.png"
        alt="Phase diagram of the Standard Model vacuum across the Higgs-top mass plane. Green marks absolute stability, yellow metastability, red instability, and black the non-perturbative region; grey contours show constant RG-improved action, and the star marks the experimental point (125.1, 173.1) GeV inside the metastable region."
        width={1600}
        height={1384}
        label="Fig. 1"
        caption="Stability of the SM vacuum across the full (M_h, M_t) plane (RG-improved Fubini-Lipatov estimator, 0.5 GeV resolution). The star marks the experimental point (125.1, 173.1) GeV, which lies in the metastable region near the stability boundary; the black dots along that boundary are the documented ansatz-breakdown artifact, not physical instabilities. Grey contours: constant RG-improved action. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/fig-phenomenological-zoom.png"
        alt="Zoom of the stability phase diagram onto the phenomenologically relevant window at 0.25 GeV resolution, with the PDG 2022 one- and two-sigma regions and constant-action contours."
        width={1600}
        height={1280}
        label="Fig. 2"
        caption="The phenomenologically relevant window at 0.25 GeV resolution with the PDG 2022 one- and two-sigma region and constant-action contours. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="analytical-vs-rg-improved" aria-labelledby="avsi-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="avsi-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">05</span> Analytical vs. RG-improved</h2>
        <p>
          The RG-improved action can be benchmarked against the strict analytical conformal limit
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm approx} = 8\\pi^2 / (3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} />. The comparison is
          between two approximations, not a correctness statement. Deep in the metastable region
          the two agree; near the stability boundary the RG improvement is substantial: the
          fractional difference
          <Math tex={'(S_{\\rm exact} - S_{\\rm approx})/S_{\\rm approx}'} /> grows from
          essentially zero at <Math tex={'(115, 180)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> to about
          <strong>+30%</strong> at <Math tex={'(134.75, 176.5)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, and the two
          estimators classify points differently right at the boundary.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/fig-classification-difference.png"
        alt="Classification difference map over the full Higgs-top plane: where the strict conformal estimate and the RG-improved evaluation assign different stability classes. Differences concentrate along the classification boundaries."
        width={1600}
        height={1384}
        label="Fig. 3"
        caption="Classification difference map: where the strict conformal estimate and the RG-improved evaluation disagree over the full plane. Differences concentrate along the classification boundaries. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/fig-action-fractional-difference.png"
        alt="Fractional difference between the RG-improved and strict conformal actions over the metastable region, growing to about thirty percent near the stability boundary and vanishing deep in the metastable region."
        width={1600}
        height={1176}
        label="Fig. 4"
        caption="Fractional difference (S_exact - S_approx)/S_approx over the metastable region: the RG improvement changes the action by about 30% near the stability boundary and is negligible deep in the metastable region. Figure from SMVac-Research."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="validation" aria-labelledby="validation-heading">
      <h2 id="validation-heading"><span class="secno">06</span> Validation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Literature boundary.</strong> The computed absolute-stability boundary sits at
          <Math tex={'M_h^{\\rm crit} = 129.2\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> for
          <Math tex={'M_t = 173.1\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, within
          <Math tex={'0.6\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> of the published NNLO value of Buttazzo et al.
          (2013); the comparison is enforced as a band check in the test suite.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Test suite.</strong> Eleven tests span unit checks of the numerics and matching,
          physics tests against analytic limits and the literature boundary, and regression tests
          against frozen reference tables (<code>reference/v1.1/</code>).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resolution convergence.</strong> Committed datasets at
          <Math tex={'0.5'} />, <Math tex={'0.1'} />, and <Math tex={'0.05\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />
          come with a convergence-check script; every classification pixel corresponds to an
          actually evaluated grid point, with no interpolation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reproducibility.</strong> Datasets, figures, and reference tables are all
          regenerable from the committed scripts; nothing depends on machine-specific state.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
      <h2 id="limitations-heading"><span class="secno">07</span> Limitations</h2>
      <p>Stated plainly, because they bound what the results mean:</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem;">
          The consolidated list in the repository separates what
          <em>affects classification</em> from what <em>affects rates only</em>; the
          absolute-stability boundary is the robust output either way. The most important items:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No bounce-equation solve (affects rates only).</strong> The conformal profile is
            a variational ansatz with only its scale optimized, so actions are <em>upper bounds</em>
            on the true bounce action: decay rates are underestimated and lifetimes
            overestimated. Only the qualitative topology of the phase diagram and the stability
            boundary should be taken over from this code.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>High-field potential (affects interpretation).</strong> The tree-level mass term
            is dropped, so the false vacuum sits at the origin rather than at the electroweak
            vacuum; the action describes tunneling from the origin as a proxy.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Assumed regularization.</strong> The Planck-suppressed
            <Math tex={'\\phi^6'} /> term is hard-coded with coefficient
            <Math tex={'c_6 = 1'} />; no literature provenance has been established for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Prefactor approximation (affects rates only).</strong> The decay-rate prefactor
            uses the <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> zero-crossing scale; its radius dependence is
            neglected (cf. Andreassen, Frost &amp; Schwartz 2018).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Retained 4-loop term.</strong> A 4-loop <Math tex={'g_3'} /> beta-function term
            with an untraceable coefficient is kept as-is; its measured impact is about
            <Math tex={'0.1\\%'} />.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="code" aria-labelledby="code-heading">
      <h2 id="code-heading"><span class="secno">08</span> Code</h2>
      <p>
        The current research codebase, SMVac-Research (v2.0.0), is a CMake C++17 project: a
        library (RGE, effective potential, bounce, numerics), command-line apps for single-point
        benchmarks and phase-diagram scans, an eleven-test suite, committed example datasets, and
        scripts that regenerate every figure from the committed data with no interpolation. The
        original working repository remains preserved unchanged as the provenance record; every
        physics function's migration is documented in the repository.
      </p>
      <pre class="codeblock"><code>git clone https://github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac.git
cd SMVac
cmake -S . -B build && cmake --build build
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
./build/benchmark_point 125.1 173.1</code></pre>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
        If you use SMVac, please cite it via the repository's
        <a href="https://github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac/blob/main/CITATION.cff" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CITATION.cff</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="future" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading"><span class="secno">09</span> Future work</h2>
      <p>
        The documented limitations mark the natural next steps: lifting the variational bound with
        a full bounce-equation solve, a decay-rate prefactor that retains the radius dependence,
        and a literature-provenance check of the regularization coefficient.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
